                <pb facs="0574" n="476" sameAs="frus:frus1920v01/pg476"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.00/3523: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in China</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Crane</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Peking</hi></placeName> [<date
                            ana="#date_undated-inferred-from-document-content" calendar="gregorian"
                            notAfter="1920-10-06T06:34:00-04:00"
                            notBefore="1920-09-27T17:00:00+08:00"><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >undated</hi></date>].<lb/>[Received October 6, 1920—6:34
                        a.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>297. Following telegram has been received from Canton: <quote rend="blockquote">
                        <p>“September 27, 5 p.m. General Lee Fu Lin, commander of troops Island of
                            Honam opposite Canton, and General Wei Pang Ping, commissioner of
                            defense Canton, having together about 6,000 troops, have rebelled
                            against the military government and threatening to attack Military
                            Governor Mo unless he resigns and leaves Canton. Mo having but 6,000
                            troops at Canton is now discussing terms. Situation critical but
                            foreigners amply protected. Two American and three British gunboats and
                            two Japanese destroyers off Shameen. Kindly inform the consuls adding
                            that I have delayed taking leave. Bergholz ”</p>
                    </quote></p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Crane</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
